    "lab-2026-11-21-chip-native-window-run": {
      "id": "0enta50380qkg47vg4ywwqadvr",
      "created": "2026-11-21T02:29:42Z",
      "hash": "sha256:4c32b0840a8395a5cb7c3b19b61a3ca8aa65488e6f91bb6a8e028b919c84379e",
      "kind": "thread",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "slug": "lab-2026-11-21-chip-native-gap-one-window",
          "id": "3nsrjj02719jqets76w1z0x8p6",
          "version": 2,
          "html": "<div class=\"blyg-tk-gen\">\n<p><strong>A first rerun of the chip-native nowcaster falls short</strong></p>\n<p>A &quot;chip-native&quot; rain nowcaster draws its forecast&#39;s random variations from a simulated thermodynamic chip, scale by scale. On one rainy 256 × 256-pixel patch of public radar data, an hour ahead, it closed 16% of the gap between a plain noise ensemble and STEPS, the method weather services use. We had set 50% in advance as the bar, so it fell short.</p>\n<p>How sure: one patch, one seed, three forecasts, and a pixel-by-pixel score that can&#39;t see the scale structure the method relies on. This neither reproduces nor refutes <a href=\"https://www.kinocomputing.com/thermodynamic-computing-doesnt-shortcut-numerical-weather-pr\">the original result</a>. <a href=\"https://github.com/msmsim/kinocomputing-lab/blob/main/experiments/e001-chip-native-nowcast/RESULTS.md\">Code and full results</a>.</p>\n</div>"
        }
      ],
